  <Placemark>
    <name>Galbraith Mountain [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Birch Street trailhead 131 m - north access to Galbraith Mountain, Bellingham, Washington</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>Between 65 and 70 miles of singletrack packed into just over 3000 acres of working timber land on Bellingham's doorstep, every metre of it built and maintained by the Whatcom Mountain Bike Coalition. The density is the point - the network folds back on itself so tightly that you can ride all day without repeating, and it covers everything from green machine-built flow to steep loamy jump lines and wooden features. It is one of the reasons Bellingham became a mountain-bike town, and the trails are signed and mapped at kiosks at the main entrances.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/><b>No lift.</b> Free to ride. The mountain is privately owned timber land with public recreation access managed by the City of Bellingham under contract with the WMBC, so the access depends on riders behaving. Park at the Birch Street trailhead on the north side (small, around eight cars), the lot off Samish Way opposite Galbraith Lane, or Lake Padden Park to the south. Riders commonly shuttle themselves up the logging roads.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Year round. It is the Pacific Northwest, so winter riding is wet - the loam drains well but expect mud, and stay off trails that are actively closed for logging.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Bellingham</td><td>Car / bike</td><td>~10 min</td></tr><tr><td>Seattle</td><td>Car</td><td>~1 h 40</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.wmbcmtb.org/galbraith">Whatcom Mountain Bike Coalition - Galbraith</a> and <a href="https://cob.org/services/recreation/parks-trails/parks-guide/galbraith-mountain">City of Bellingham - Galbraith Mountain</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
