  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Kingdom Trails (Vermont) [15 USD]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Kingdom Trails Welcome Center 252 m - hub of the Kingdom Trails network, East Burke, Vermont</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>About 110 miles of trail run by the non-profit Kingdom Trails Association, and the network that more or less invented the New England trail-town model. The character changes with the ground: mellow, fast, meadow-top singletrack on Darling Hill, and rooty technical descents in the forest below. Much of it crosses private land held open by landowner agreement, which is why the day pass and the etiquette are not optional - the network has closed sections before when that broke down. Burke Mountain Resort next door runs a separate lift-served bike park.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/><b>No lift.</b> A 15 USD day pass is required to ride, sold at the Welcome Center and at Baileys and Burke in the village. Seasonal passes are cheaper per day if you are staying.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Roughly May to October, depending on the thaw and the autumn rains. Check the association's trail status page - trails close when they are too wet.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>East Burke</td><td>Walk / bike</td><td>In the village</td></tr><tr><td>St Johnsbury</td><td>Car</td><td>~30 min</td></tr><tr><td>Burlington</td><td>Car</td><td>~1 h 30</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Source: <a href="https://www.kingdomtrails.org/">Kingdom Trails Association</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
