  <Placemark>
    <name>Tiger Mountain MTB [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Tiger Summit trailhead 420 m - SR-18 access to the Tiger Mountain trails, Washington</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>The closest serious riding to Seattle, on Washington DNR land in the Tiger Mountain State Forest, with the trails built and looked after by Evergreen Mountain Bike Alliance. Northwest Timber starts from this trailhead by way of a short connector and is the gentle introduction; Preston Railroad is a long cross-country descent on an old grade, easier than its neighbours and good for less confident riders; Off the Grid and East Tiger Summit are the faster, more built descents, with a view to Mount Rainier where the trees allow. Master Link and Quick Link are uphill-only and are how you reach the East Tiger summit - respect the direction, this is a busy forest.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/><b>No lift.</b> Climb the uphill-only trails or the forest roads. The trails are free, but a Washington Discover Pass is required to park at the trailhead.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Year round, though it is wet from autumn to spring and some trails close seasonally or for logging - check Evergreen's trail status before driving out.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Issaquah</td><td>Car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Seattle</td><td>Car</td><td>~40 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Sources: <a href="https://www.evergreenmtb.org/trails/tiger-mtn">Evergreen Mountain Bike Alliance - Tiger Mountain</a> and <a href="https://www.dnr.wa.gov/Tiger">Washington DNR - Tiger Mountain State Forest</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
