  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Lunch Loops [no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Tabeguache / Lunch Loops trailhead 1437 m - main access to the Lunch Loops, Grand Junction, Colorado</b><br/><br/>
    <b>Trails</b><br/>A stacked-loop system on BLM ground two miles from downtown Grand Junction, developed by the Grand Junction BLM office with IMBA, and named for being close enough to ride on a lunch break. The riding is technical desert singletrack through multi-coloured rock - constant step-ups, drop moves, steep pitches and fast descents. Free Lunch is the expert line, with a series of drops and a genuinely rowdy character; Holy Cross, Gunny Loop and Eagle's Wing are advanced to expert, with Gunny Loop climbing about 90 m to the high point of the network before giving back around 240 m. There are easier loops off the same trailhead, but the system's reputation is built on the hard ones.<br/><br/>
    <b>Getting up / price</b><br/><b>No lift.</b> Free, on BLM land, with a large trailhead car park.<br/><br/>
    <b>Open season</b><br/>Spring and autumn are best. Summer is hot enough that riding early matters, and the trails should be left alone when the desert clay is wet.<br/><br/>
    <b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Grand Junction</td><td>Car / bike, Monument Road</td><td>~10 min</td></tr><tr><td>Fruita</td><td>Car</td><td>~25 min</td></tr></table><br/>
    <i>Source: <a href="https://www.blm.gov/programs/recreation/mountainbike/lunchloops">Bureau of Land Management - Lunch Loops</a>. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</i>]]></description>
